{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_09-22-17-omnipaxos-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor it in s['interpretations']:\n    if it['id'] in ('cb1eff22546b408ab3c70688d9281b42-interpretation-0','86c29f4a64e54bcaae185361134b8847-interpretation-0'):\n        print(\\\"=====\\\", it['id'])\n        print(\\\"SOURCE_IDS\\\", it['source_ids'])\n        print(\\\"EXEC\\\", it['executions'])\n        print(\\\"RATIONALE\\\", it['rationale'])\n        print(\\\"COUNTER\\\", json.dumps(it['counterevidence'], indent=0))\n        print(\\\"LIMITS\\\", json.dumps(it['limitations'], indent=0))\n        print(\\\"JOINT\\\", it['joint_history'])\n        print(\\\"ANS\\\", it.get('answers'))\n        print(\\\"KNOW\\\", json.dumps(it.get('knowledge')))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"===== cb1eff22546b408ab3c70688d9281b42-interpretation-0\nSOURCE_IDS ['trim_derives_from_min_accepted', 'get_min_all_accepted_idx', 'trim_requires_all_decided_doc', 'memory_storage_trim', 'sync_log_accounting', 'create_log_sync', 'send_accsync_valid_entries', 'memory_storage_read_helpers', 'snapshot_decided_bound_doc', 'try_snapshot_decided_bound', 'create_snapshot_range']\nEXEC {'ec3329dc273e452aa92bcd44652c116a': [9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15]}\nRATIONALE Witnessed and bounded, now with a second producer recorded. The trim witness stands: with recorded accepted indexes drifted to 10, 9 and 7 over six indices of content and every node's decided report at 9, trim(None) returned no error and compacted index 7, past the leader's own content, after which its reads of indices 5 and 6 reported Trimmed (ec3329dc273e452aa92bcd44652c116a events 9-15). The discriminator this question carried asked whether a producer other than trim can raise a compacted index past a replica's content, and the snapshot receipt answers that it can: with the same drifted state, snapshot(Some(8), false) returned Ok, advanced the leader's compacted index to 8 and produced a snapshot holding none of the five decided entries (e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c events 0-5). That second producer is stricter in one respect and looser in another than trim: it needs no recorded accepted index at all - its bound is the leader's decided index - so a drift confined to the decided index would be enough for it, while trim additionally needs the drifted value to be the configuration's minimum recorded accepted index. What remains bounded from the earlier reading: both producers are bounded by values the leader derives for itself, so a replica whose recorded report is still truthful keeps trim's bound honest (the minimum) and the snapshot witness also required the leader's own decided index to have drifted; and no in-cluster promise or log sync in the observed runs asked for a prefix below a compacted index, so the storage underflow I had sourced from the lead was not triggered.\nCOUNTER [\n\"Every node's decided report had also drifted to 9, so the documented all-decided precondition was satisfied only in the drifted bookkeeping; the obligation I rely on is that the compacted index must not exceed the entries the leader holds, which it did (7 for trim, 8 for the snapshot).\",\n\"The trim call returned Ok, so nothing in its own result signals the over-compaction; the witness is the compacted index together with the content sampled before and after.\",\n\"The crash path is argued unreachable in this configuration rather than merely untested: the trim bound is the minimum recorded accepted index, so every replica's recorded value is at or above the compacted index and no in-cluster suffix request can fall below it.\"\n]\nLIMITS [\n\"The trim over-compaction is bounded to replicas whose recorded accepted index has drifted; a truthful replica would have kept the bound lower.\",\n\"The underflow remainder is a property of a storage or caller that requests a prefix below the compacted index by another route; other storage implementations may differ and the trait leaves out-of-range behaviour to the implementer.\",\n\"The baseline comparisons of both receipts are unknown because they were admitted through event names shared across cases in one execution; those states are asserted from the sampled operands.\",\n\"One three-node configuration, three reconnection rounds, one leader change (trim) and one snapshot-capable entry type were exercised; forwarded compaction on the peers was not sampled beyond their accepted and decided indexes.\"\n]\nJOINT This account's own receipt is ec3329dc273e452aa92bcd44652c116a from experiment 1e6c241b318a46a390243865236b7eb0-experiment, whose drift-then-trim case the account cites. The second producer it refers to was sampled in a sibling investigation of the same drift parent: receipt e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c from experiment a9cad483efbe4868a0d1c52a13253499-experiment (the snapshot child), which reproduced the same drift with the snapshot-capable entry type and then called snapshot(Some(8), false); the two receipts are read on their own samples and no measurement is combined across them.\nANS {}\nKNOW [{\"entry_id\": \"4c57d4fe287e4534a689e56689def6a8-knowledge-0\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"trim accepted a drifted bound and compacted past the content the leader held\"}, {\"entry_id\": \"856d6fe2b3a14c7ab6dbed00026ba199-knowledge-0\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"the snapshot path accepted a drifted bound and produced a snapshot holding none of the entries\"}, {\"entry_id\": \"76cabb0efc3b4eda8eda8c9c79025550-knowledge-0\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"trim's bound is the minimum recorded accepted index, so a truthful replica keeps it honest\"}]\n===== 86c29f4a64e54bcaae185361134b8847-interpretation-0\nSOURCE_IDS ['snapshot_decided_bound_doc', 'try_snapshot_decided_bound', 'create_snapshot_range', 'internal_read_range', 'memory_storage_read_helpers', 'create_log_sync', 'sync_log_accounting', 'no_stopsign_decided_idx', 'trim_derives_from_min_accepted']\nEXEC {'e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c': [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]}\nRATIONALE Witnessed, with the discriminator's variants now settled by source. Witness: with the drift in place (accepted 9, 9 and 6, decided 9, six indices of content), snapshot(Some(8), false) returned Ok on the leader, its compacted index became 8, and the snapshot reachable at index 0 held zero entry ids where the log had five decided entries (e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c events 0-5); the comparisons requiring the compacted index to reach six and the snapshot to hold five entries were violated at the call event. Variants: (i) a request equal to the drifted decided index is not protected, because the equal case maps it to the decided log without the StopSign (8) which still exceeds the content (6) - this corrects my earlier counter-argument; (ii) the bound is computed on whichever node receives the call, so a follower with the same drifted decided index accepts the same request, and the local_only flag only decides whether the request is forwarded; (iii) the forwarded form repeats the derivation on every peer - each peer's own snapshot call compares the requested index against its own decided index (which in the observed run was also 9) and builds from its own range, so the peers end in the same loss rather than a different state, while the sampling here covered only their accepted and decided indexes (events 4, 5); (iv) the form the loss takes is whatever the entry type's Snapshot::create gives to an empty slice, which for this entry type is an empty snapshot that the reads then report for the compacted region.\nCOUNTER [\n\"The call itself returned Ok, so nothing in its result signals the loss; the witness is the compacted index together with the number of entries the produced snapshot holds.\",\n\"The user supplied the index 8; the documented note allows any index up to the decided index, which was 9, so the request was inside the documented allowance rather than a caller error.\",\n\"The apparent protection offered by the equal case does not hold in the observed state: it lowers the bound from 9 to 8, which is still above the content (6).\"\n]\nLIMITS [\n\"The consequence depends on the entry type's snapshot: here the snapshot records its input entries and is empty for an empty slice; an entry type that reconstructs state differently could behave differently.\",\n\"The peers' own snapshots were not sampled in this run; the claim that they end in the same loss is read from the derivation that each peer computes its own bound and range, not from their observed snapshots.\",\n\"The follower-side, equal-index and peer-side variants are argued from the derivation rather than executed; only the leader's call was sampled.\",\n\"One three-node configuration, three reconnection rounds, one index and one entry type were exercised, and the comparisons tying the before and after samples to their admissions are unknown because of a shared event name in the same execution, so those states are asserted from the sampled operands (events 0-5).\"\n]\nJOINT Same harness lineage as the drift account: receipt e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c ran experiment a9cad483efbe4868a0d1c52a13253499-experiment, whose snap case reproduces receipt fd0b8be59c8f466fbf7768abb08f93b5 and whose new case uses the same snapshot-capable entry type and the same three reconnection rounds as the trim receipt ec3329dc273e452aa92bcd44652c116a; each case is read on its own samples and no measurement is combined across executions.\nANS {}\nKNOW [{\"entry_id\": \"27a729f8adb440c2b845ef833c3a16ec-knowledge-0\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"the snapshot bound comes from the decided index alone\"}, {\"entry_id\": \"856d6fe2b3a14c7ab6dbed00026ba199-knowledge-0\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"the snapshot path accepted a drifted bound and produced a snapshot holding none of the entries\"}, {\"entry_id\": \"e65d63f4057d4c5bbe8390ac06b30fce-knowledge-2\", \"revision\": 1, \"assertion\": \"the recorded accepted and decided indexes drift one index per re-synchronization round\"}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
